 The initial connector enrollment implementation preserves the original bootstrap/key on retries, durably audits the claim before signing, and caps provider calls at two nonrefundable attempts of at most five seconds each. Issued identities have a fixed expiry thirty minutes after the original claim. Unknown signing outcomes do not become peer authority. Signed results require an exact durable encrypted audit and a current atomic peer registration before delivery; retry cannot re-enable revoked registration or extend any deadline. See the [activation contract](../docs/architecture/enrollment-activation.md). [Protected fleet administration](../docs/architecture/fleet-administration.md) assigns enrollment separately from workload access. [Credential delivery](../docs/architecture/bootstrap-channel.md) uses a configured secret-channel profile, returns no secret in operation results and cannot activate a slot before issuance audit. These refinements do not qualify production enrollment merely by being specified.
 
 For the optional fresh-install observation-bootstrap profile, the original protected owner Service UID and issuer scope derive one common TLS verification name alongside each server leaf's existing exact process name. Before dialing the installed reachable endpoint, the consumer MUST retain a finite current independently accepted owner candidate set. Standard TLS hostname/root verification, membership of the actual leaf in that set and exact current SQL activation/configuration MUST precede peer-proof exchange. Only the producer's own scoped source/guard/route facts may pass before full proof; workload metadata and effects require the complete current proof. Original deadlines are never restarted at receipt. Existing exact-owner/public/effect routes MUST reject common-SNI connections, including requests that substitute an exact-owner HTTP Host. Omitted capability preserves the legacy profile; this is not a rolling upgrade or protection against a compromised authorized cluster-local observer. See [ADR144](../docs/decisions/144-cluster-local-observations.md).
+
+[ADR-155](../docs/decisions/155-unchanged-fleet-persistence-contention.md) permits only a classified failed persistence of an independently observed unchanged positive fleet source to retain its previous original SQL expiry without an additional suspension. Classification MUST require the exact last LockFleetTrust55P03 abort before UPDATE/COMMIT and complete current source/row/database-incarnation comparison before and after blocking work. It MUST remain unavailable and MUST invalidate scheduling notices, create no write/success hint and never refresh a selected proof. Source-read errors, new/changed/suspended/recovery state, expiry, failed confirmation and uncertain commits MUST retain ordinary fencing. The next observation MUST be independently selected under normal cadence; no timeout, role, lock or retry bound changes follow.
 
